!! Device controller module for RBR — drives Zigbee radiator relays.
!!
!! Sits between the main controller (controller.allspeak) and the relay devices, translating per-room commands into HTTP calls on the local Zigbee bridge.
!!
!! Run as a sub-module of controller.allspeak (via `run ... as DeviceModule`) — communication with the parent is by EasyCoder messaging, not MQTT. The parent stops the module by terminating; there is no explicit shutdown handshake.
!!
!! The script starts by declaring all the variables it uses, registers the on-message handler that routes incoming RoomSpecs, and signals the parent it is ready.
!   deviceControl.allspeak - a script to drive radiator relays and read temperatures

    script DeviceControl
    
    dictionary RoomSpec
    dictionary Response
    dictionary Values
    list Relays
    list Replies
    variable RoomName
    variable RelayName
    variable RelayState
    variable URL
    variable Reply
    variable R
    variable TrvName
    variable TrvSetpoint
    variable TrvMode

!    debug step
    
    ! Comms between this module and the controller is done with EasyCoder messaging (not MQTT)
    log `Set up the device controller`
    on message go to RunController
    release parent
    log `Device controller is ready`
    stop
!! @hash 5a913289
!! @verified 5a913289
!!!
!! Top-level message handler — routes a RoomSpec to the Zigbee bridge.
!!
!! Invoked by the EasyCoder runtime whenever controller.allspeak sends us a RoomSpec via `send ... to DeviceModule`.
!!
!! Two message shapes are accepted:
!!
!! Request relay (boiler demand): RoomSpec has a `request` entry naming the relay and a `relay state` (`on`/`off`). The request/demand relay drives the boiler and lives on the same bridge as the room relays. We send the requested state to that single device and reply with a single-element list.
!!
!! Room relay batch: RoomSpec carries `room name`, `relays` (one or more relay names — most rooms have one but some rooms control multiple radiators in parallel) and `relay state`. Each named relay is dispatched to MessageZigbeeDevice and every response is collected into Replies before the whole list goes back.
!!
!! The reply list lets controller.allspeak track each relay individually: an empty entry counts as a failure (incremented into the room's `relayfails`), and a populated entry carries the state the device reported back.
!!
!! MessageZigbeeDevice reads RelayState (not Message) to build its URL. Each branch ends with `stop` rather than `return` because we are the registered on-message handler — `stop` returns control to the runtime to await the next message without re-entering the script body.
RunController:
!    log `RunController:` cat the message
    put the message into RoomSpec

    ! A room with a self-contained valve sends the setpoint and mode instead
    ! of an on/off relay command. The bridge queues it; a battery valve may
    ! take until its next wake to apply it.
    if RoomSpec has entry `trv`
    begin
        put entry `trv` of RoomSpec into TrvName
        put entry `setpoint` of RoomSpec into TrvSetpoint
        put entry `mode` of RoomSpec into TrvMode
        gosub to MessageTrv
        reset Replies
        append Reply to Replies
        send Replies to sender
        stop
    end

    ! See if this room is the request relay. The request/demand relay drives
    ! the boiler and so lives on the same bridge as the room relays.
    ! MessageZigbeeDevice reads RelayState (not Message) to build its URL,
    ! so populate it to match the room-relay path below.
    if RoomSpec has entry `request`
    begin
        put entry `request` of RoomSpec into RelayName
        put entry `relay state` of RoomSpec into RelayState
        gosub to MessageZigbeeDevice
        reset Replies
        append Reply to Replies
        send Replies to sender
        stop
    end

    ! No, so do a regular room
    put entry `room name` of RoomSpec into RoomName
    put entry `relays` of RoomSpec into Relays
    put entry `relay state` of RoomSpec into RelayState
    if RelayState is empty set RelayState to `off`

    reset Replies
    set R to 0
    while R is less than the count of Relays
    begin
        put item R of Relays into RelayName
!        log `Do relay ` cat RelayName cat ` in ` cat RoomName
        gosub to MessageZigbeeDevice
        append Reply to Replies
        increment R
    end
    send Replies to sender
    stop
!! @hash 75d56619
!! @verified 75d56619
!!!
!! Send a relay command to a Zigbee device via the local zigbee-bridge.
!!
!! The bridge listens on 127.0.0.1:8889, translates HTTP to MQTT for zigbee2mqtt, and returns the device's reported state as JSON. URL form is `http://127.0.0.1:8889/device/<name>?state=<on|off>`. The device name is the friendly name registered with zigbee2mqtt and must match the relay name in map.json.
!!
!! On success we build a small Values dictionary carrying the device's reported `state` (so the controller can confirm the relay actually flipped) and a placeholder `uptime` of 0, then return that as Reply. On bridge failure (network error, bridge not running) or a response with no `state` field, Reply is left empty so the caller counts it as a relay failure.
!!
!! No address setup is required: the bridge always runs on localhost, and a device is identified solely by its zigbee2mqtt friendly name.
MessageZigbeeDevice:
    put `http://127.0.0.1:8889/device/` cat RelayName cat `?state=` cat RelayState into URL
!    log URL
    get Response from url URL
    or begin
        log `Zigbee bridge call failed for ` cat RelayName
        set Reply to empty
        return
    end
!    log RelayName cat ` ` cat Response
    reset Values
    set entry `uptime` of Values to 0
    if Response has entry `state`
    begin
        set entry `state` of Values to entry `state` of Response
        put Values into Reply
    end
    else set Reply to empty
    return
!! @hash 1f26f342
!! @verified 1f26f342
!!!
!! Send a setpoint/mode command to a self-contained TRV through the bridge.
!!
!! The bridge publishes to zigbee2mqtt and returns immediately with
!! `accepted`/`queued`; a battery valve is asleep and may apply the command
!! only when it next checks in, so there is no synchronous state confirmation
!! to wait for. A missing `accepted` is a failure.
MessageTrv:
    put `http://127.0.0.1:8889/trv/` cat TrvName cat `?setpoint=` cat TrvSetpoint cat `&mode=` cat TrvMode into URL
    get Response from url URL
    or begin
        log `Zigbee TRV call failed for ` cat TrvName
        set Reply to empty
        return
    end
    reset Values
    if Response has entry `accepted`
    begin
        set entry `accepted` of Values to entry `accepted` of Response
        if Response has entry `queued` set entry `queued` of Values to entry `queued` of Response
        if Response has entry `setpoint` set entry `setpoint` of Values to entry `setpoint` of Response
        if Response has entry `mode` set entry `mode` of Values to entry `mode` of Response
        put Values into Reply
    end
    else
    begin
        log `TRV ` cat TrvName cat ` not accepted`
        set Reply to empty
    end
    return
